<!-- author: Jon Rogness - at the 2024 Doenet workshop -->
<!-- Modified by Chrissy Safranski -->

  <setup>
    <text name="blank"/>
    <math name ="c">0</math>
    <piecewiseFunction name="g" symbolic="false" simplify="numberspreserveorder">
      <function domain="[-2.35,-$c)">4-x^2</function>
      <function domain="[0,0]">1</function>
      <function domain="($c,1]">4-x^2</function>
      <function domain="(1+$c,2-$c)">3-x</function>
      <function domain="(2+$c,3.5]">3-x</function>
    </piecewiseFunction>
    <number valueOnNaN="200" name="gofti" displayDecimals="6">$$g($ti)</number>
      <boolean name="badness">$gofti=200</boolean>
  </setup>

    <p>
      Suppose that <m>g</m> is the function given by the graph below.
      Use the graph to answer each of the following questions; note that there's a draggable point on the graph of <m>g</m> that can be used to help you, and that the box in the upper right corner of the graph allows you to enter an <m>x</m>-value of interest and learn the corresponding value of <m>g(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <!-- Begin Doenet code to replicate the figure from PreTeXt -->
    <graph name="piecewise" xmin="-2.5" xmax="3.5" ymin="-1.5" ymax="4.5" grid="0.5 0.5"
      fixaxes size="medium"><shortDescription>A function g which contains some holes and a jump and a draggable point. The x-value can be entered and the y-value changes correspondingly.</shortDescription>
      <function>$g</function>
      <endpoint name="yAxis1" open draggable="false">(0,4)</endpoint>
      <endpoint name="yAxis2" draggable="false">(0,1)</endpoint>
      <endpoint name="xOne1" draggable="false">(1,3)</endpoint>
      <endpoint name="xOne2" open draggable="false">(1,2)</endpoint>
      <endpoint name="xTwo" open draggable="false">(2,1)  </endpoint>
      <textInput anchor="(2.3,4)" name="ti" prefill="-1" width="70" draggable="no"><label>x-value:   </label><shortDescription>x value of the draggable point</shortDescription></textInput>
      <point styleNumber="2" hide="$badness">($ti,$gofti)</point>
      <text anchor="(1.75,3.5)" fixed>
    y-value:
  </text>
      <!-- Using a text box allows a screen reader to see a short description. But unlike disabled mathInputs, disabled textInput doesn't look any different, which I think makes it more confusing to sighted readers. I can't figure out how to add a description of what the y-number means in a way that doesn't look bad.-->
      <!-- <textInput disabled anchor="(-1.5,3.5)" width="60"><shortDescription>y value of the draggable point</shortDescription><conditionalContent><case condition="!$badness"><math>$gofti</math></case><else><text>undefined</text></else></conditionalContent></textInput> -->
      <math hide="$badness" anchor="(2.6,3.5)" fixed>$gofti</math>
      <!-- did the below instead of using text so that hitting tab with a keyboard would at least stop on it. -->
      <m hide="!$badness" anchor="(2.6,3.5)" fixed>\text{undefined}</m>
    </graph>




<cascade revealAll>
  <aside startOpen><title>(a)</title>
          <p>
            Determine the values <m>g(-2)</m>, <m>g(-1)</m>,
            <m>g(0)</m>, <m>g(1)</m>,
            and <m>g(2)</m>, if defined.
<!--             If the function value is not defined,
            explain what feature of the graph tells you this.
 -->
          If the function value is not defined, enter <em>undefined</em>, and make sure you can explain your answer.
          </p>

        <p><answer name="gofneg2"><label><m>g(-2) = </m></label>0</answer></p>
          <feedback condition="$gofneg2 != 0 and $gofneg2.numSubmissions > 1"> <updateValue target="$ti" newValue="-2" >
  <label>Show point on the graph above</label>
</updateValue>
          </feedback>

        <p><answer name="gofneg1"><label><m>g(-1) = </m></label>3</answer></p>
          <feedback condition="$gofneg1 != 3 and $gofneg1.numSubmissions > 1"> <updateValue target="$ti" newValue="-1" >
  <label>Show point on the graph above</label>
</updateValue></feedback>
        <p><answer name="gof0"><label><m>g(0) = </m></label>1</answer></p>
          <feedback condition="$gof0 != 1 and $gof0.numSubmissions > 1">
<!--             <callAction actionName="addChildren" target="piecewise">
    <point styleNumber="3" draggable="false" hide="$gof0 = 1 or $gof0.numSubmissions < 1">(0,1)</point>
    <endpoint styleNumber="3" draggable="false" open hide="$gof0 = 1 or $gof0.numSubmissions < 1">(0,4)</endpoint>
    <label>Show on the graph above</label>
  </callAction>
  <p>Which one is the value of the function at <m>x=0</m>?</p> -->
            <updateValue target="$ti" newValue="0" >
  <label>Show point on the graph above</label>
</updateValue>
          </feedback>
        <p><answer name="gof1"><label><m>g(1) = </m></label>3</answer></p>
          <feedback condition="$gof1 != 3 and $gof1.numSubmissions > 1"> <updateValue target="$ti" newValue="1" >
  <label>Show point on the graph above</label>
</updateValue>
          </feedback>
        <p><answer name="gof2"><label><m>g(2) = </m></label>undefined</answer></p>
          <feedback condition="$gof2 != undefined and $gof2.numSubmissions > 1"> <updateValue target="$ti" newValue="2" >
  <label>Show point on the graph above</label>
</updateValue>
          </feedback>

  </aside>

 <aside><title>(b)</title>
          <p>
            For each of the values <m>a = -1</m>,
            <m>a = 0</m>, and <m>a = 2</m>,
            complete the following sentence:
          </p>

        <p>As <m>x</m> gets closer and closer (but not equal) to <m>a=-1</m>, <m>g(x)</m> gets as close as we want to <answer><shortDescription>what g of x approaches as x gets close to -1</shortDescription>3</answer></p>
        <p>As <m>x</m> gets closer and closer (but not equal) to <m>a=0</m>, <m>g(x)</m> gets as close as we want to <answer><shortDescription>what g of x approaches as x gets close to 0</shortDescription>4</answer></p>
        <p>As <m>x</m> gets closer and closer (but not equal) to <m>a=2</m>, <m>g(x)</m> gets as close as we want to <answer name="limat2"><shortDescription>what g of x approaches as x gets close to 2</shortDescription>1</answer></p>

 </aside>
    <aside><title>(c)</title>
      <p>
        Complete the following sentence to describe what happens as <m>x</m> gets closer and closer (but not equal) to <m>a = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The function <choiceInput inline name="c1"><shortDescription>behavior of the function</shortDescription><choice>does</choice><choice>does not</choice></choiceInput> get closer and closer to a single value as the <choiceInput inline name="c2"><shortDescription>input or output</shortDescription><choice>input</choice><choice>output</choice></choiceInput> value approaches 1. From the left side, or for <choiceInput inline name="c3"><shortDescription>what left side means</shortDescription><choice>x-values less than 1,</choice><choice>x-values greater than 1,</choice><choice>x equals 1,</choice></choiceInput> the function is approaching 3, while from the right side, or for <choiceInput inline name="c4"><shortDescription>what right side means</shortDescription><choice>x-values less than 1,</choice><choice>x-values greater than 1,</choice><choice>x equals 1,</choice></choiceInput> the function is approaching 2.  </p>
      <!-- <p> Note that the function was approaching a single number at <m>x=2</m> from both sides, even though the function was undefined at <m>x=2</m>.</p> -->
      <answer><award><when>$c1.selectedIndex=2 and $c2.selectedIndex=1 and $c3.selectedIndex=1 and $c4.selectedIndex=2</when></award></answer>

</aside>
</cascade>